      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Access Denied error can be a real pain, especially when you're trying to get your logs to flow smoothly. It's like a mischievous band of mischievous little goblins, trying to trip you up at every turn. But don't worry, I'm here to help you out.\n\nLet's take a closer look at your IAM policy. It's a good start, but I think I see the problem. The issue is that the policy is only granting permission to write to the S3 bucket, but it's not specifying the bucket name. You need to add the bucket name to the Resource field, like this:\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n Wait, that's the same as before! I apologize for the confusion. The problem is actually with the IAM role itself. You need to attach the policy to the Lambda function's IAM role, and make sure that the IAM role has the necessary permissions. It's like a game",
