    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sorry about the goblin tangent, I got a bit carried away. Let's focus on the technical side.\n\nYou're right, the policy you provided is identical to the one you initially sent, and you've already attached it to the Lambda function's IAM role. In that case, the issue might be related to the IAM role's trust relationship.\n\nWhen you create a Lambda function, AWS automatically creates an IAM role for it, which includes a trust relationship that specifies the service that's allowed to assume the role. By default, this trust relationship is set to allow AWS services to assume the role, but it doesn't explicitly grant permission to the Lambda function to write to S3.\n\nYou need to add a statement to the IAM role's trust relationship that grants permission to the Lambda function to write to S3. This statement should have the same Action and Resource as your policy, like this:\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Principal\": {\n        \"Service\": \"lambda.amazonaws.com\"\n      },\n      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\",\n      \"Resource\": \"*\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",",
